---
title: "Grouping and aggregation"
---

## The shape of a grouped query {#sec-group-shape}

`group_by` does not itself produce a frame — it produces a grouping that `agg`
turns back into one. The result has one row per distinct key combination, and one
column per aggregate you asked for.

::: {.definition}
A **grouping key** is the tuple of column values that defines one output row. Rows
sharing a key are aggregated together; the key columns are carried through to the
result unchanged.
:::

```python
by_region = sales.group_by("region").agg(
    tarn.col("revenue").sum(),
    tarn.col("order_id").count(),
)
```

Output columns are named `<column>_<aggregate>` unless you rename them with
`.alias()`. Group on several columns by passing several names.

## Aggregates that are not sums {#sec-group-aggregates}

Beyond `sum`, `mean`, `min`, `max` and `count`, two aggregates come up often enough
to name here. `n_unique` counts distinct values within the group, and `first` takes
the value from the group's first row in the frame's current order — which is only
meaningful after an explicit `sort`.

::: {.definition}
An aggregate is **order-dependent** when its result can change under a permutation of
the input rows that does not change the grouping key. `first` and `last` are
order-dependent; `sum` and `n_unique` are not.
:::

Order-dependent aggregates on an unsorted frame are the most common source of
results that differ between runs, because the reader is free to return rows in
whatever order is cheapest.

## Grouping on a derived value {#sec-group-derived}

You do not need a real column to group on. Add one first with `with_column`, then
group on its name:

```python
by_month = (
    sales.with_column(tarn.col("date").dt.month().alias("month"))
    .group_by("month")
    .agg(tarn.col("revenue").sum())
)
```
